{"author":{"id":"c1fda884d46208607117c580f4eddc8a36848ef6fd6aedbde3f7c6e5a903847c"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1779470983,"nonce":"KqNifgIB6N22QMMpP6mNyiUw+/0=","metadata":{"github-id":"IC_kwDOK4fCvM8AAAABDXvqHA","github-url":"https://github.com/rolker/unh_echoboats_project11/issues/138#issuecomment-4521191964"},"message":"Hmmm... correct me if I'm wrong, but the 13-meter gap is the geoid undulation... the difference between the WGS84 ellipsoid and the EGM96 geoid (the mean sea level approximation). NavSatFix altitude is ellipsoidal by convention, but many EKF implementations quietly assume it's orthometric (above sea level), which introduces exactly this kind of offset.\n\nThe clean fix is to fuse GPS in ECEF coordinates end-to-end rather than converting to a local ENU frame, which sidesteps the geoid/ellipsoid ambiguity entirely. [FusionCore](https://github.com/manankharwar/fusioncore) does this.... it fuses NavSatFix directly in ECEF and subtracts a reference origin, so there's no intermediate frame conversion where the altitude reference can get mixed up.","files":null}]}